WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.380
The artist is creating first, then you are supposed to serve the artist, help the artist.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:11.520
And your profit will be made because you serve them well.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:20.320
And we need to bring that selfless way of being around people back, because that's who we are.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.120
The oldest common ground we all have is stories.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:31.000
We are all humans and we all come from this storytelling place, sitting around the fire and some old man telling you the story.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.000
It doesn't matter if you're from here or there.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
All the civilizations across time have been doing this.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.000
And we talk to each other and share knowledge that way.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
More than sats, more than Bitcoin,

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.000
the most important

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
currency that we have is the time that we are given,

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.000
and we don't know how much we have.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.000
So that's a very humbling experience to me.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.000
And that pretty much

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.000
killed a big part of my ego.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.000
And I realized that you want to leave something that matters and that can help people once you're gone,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:15.000
right? So having a stroke and almost dying made me realize I should pursue Bitcoin, because I knew about Bitcoin at that point.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.600
Welcome to Share Your Bitcoin Journey, presented by Bitcoin For The Arts. I'm Dion Wilson, founder and executive director of Bitcoin For The Arts,

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:33.680
a 501(c)(3) nonprofit empowering artists with Bitcoin grants, education, and sovereign funding tools.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.440
Today we have the pleasure of speaking with a husband, a node runner, a designer,

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:49.000
an illustrator, an event producer, and an open source enthusiast. He is co-founder of Ninefold, a studio that builds worlds,

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.000
stories, products, and experiences people want to enjoy.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
One of those worlds is a retro sci-fi

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.320
animated series about an extraterrestrial trying to understand humanity through Bitcoin.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:12.280
Another is a collaboration with Timechain Art Magazine that treats Bitcoin as

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:20.160
frequency. It moved from Nostr onto a wall in Denver and into a pin you can hold.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:27.440
In 2024, a drawing of his was tattooed on a friend's leg at Nostriga.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.280
In 2025, that drawing became a Bitcoin pin.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:38.800
In this conversation, he shares his personal journey from maker to node runner to studio

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.120
founder,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:45.000
why a story can teach proof of work better than a lecture,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.120
and what it means to sell culture peer to peer

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:50.280
in sats.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.120
His story is handmade, open,

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.120
and built to travel from a screen to a wall to a pin.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.860
Without further ado, welcome to the show, Fzero.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:04.840
Thank you, Dion.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.240
Thank you for this introduction.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.640
Happy to be here and thank you for having me.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.000
Fzero, welcome to Share Your Bitcoin Journey.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.080
Before Ninefold, before the series, before the pins,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.880
there was a person making pictures.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.080
Where did that story actually begin for you?

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.040
I would say that I started drawing very young. When I was a kid, I was

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:33.680
really into manga and Japanese animation in general, so that's why I started. I was really into

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.440
the story and the way you can build images and make them go one after the other and then

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:45.280
animate them and create something bigger, right? And then you have the song and the sound effects and

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.280
everything coming in. So I was really fascinated by that, because I'm very visual to start with.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.720
And then growing up, I wasn't really pursuing art anymore. I was just at school and

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:04.800
just being a kid trying to grow up. And eventually I ended up studying economics, finance,

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.360
marketing.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.480
I went in full on.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.080
I went to a job.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.720
I worked for big retail fashion brands.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.560
I was opening shops for them.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.480
I ended up in daily operations as a store manager.

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.040
So you deal with people.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.720
You deal with everything.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.160
And as I was doing this, my career was looking great,

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.580
but I wasn't very inspired.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.320
So I remember the art part was missing,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.280
and that's where I started

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.080
reconnecting with drawing and tattooing,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.320
which is my first love.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:42.720
You call yourself a designer and an illustrator.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:47.120
What were you making before Bitcoin, and who taught you to

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:54.160
look that closely? So just like I said, retail fashion. If you want to handle the store, you have to

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:59.600
be looking at every detail, because you cannot miss anything. You cannot have a box full of items

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:06.640
left in the back, and then it's value sitting there. And you have to manage time and everyone. So you have to be

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:14.800
very detail oriented and manage expectations. So that's how I came up with standards for myself,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:19.920
discipline for myself. So it starts with yourself, I believe. But once you've done that, then you can

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:29.680
also apply it to your environment and make the environment better, and then also lead people to pay attention to those details that they don't even see, they don't even imagine

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.520
they exist, right? So when I was doing this, I saw people opening their eyes and

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.040
becoming more interested in the fiat context, right? And then I realized that maybe there

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.360
is a way to do the same thing but have a better impact, and maybe share a story that is

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.680
more connected to my values.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.480
So you try to put yourself in a position where you can make people really notice the details of

00:05:52.480 --> 00:06:00.560
the work? Yes, because I believe that everything is based on symbolism, and what we think we see is

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:07.720
basically the big picture, or some big picture in general. And we don't really look closer, which

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.200
leaves a lot of meaning on the table.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:11.960
And if we don't have the meaning,

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:13.400
and we don't have the common ground,

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.120
then we are not using the same words for the same reasons.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.600
And then when I'm saying a word to you,

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.480
yeah, maybe you're understanding something,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.840
but I mean something else.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:39.000
So I really want to take the time to educate and explain the things I want to explain my way, and I want to meet you and hear what you think about it and just exchange ideas, and I believe that together we can come up with a better solution.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Most makers can name the piece where they thought, this is the work.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.000
What was that piece for you?

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:54.000
As I told you, I had just started thinking about quitting my job, my fiat job,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.000
and I was going back to drawing, and I was only doing black and white

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.000
because of my background in tattooing, so I think in lines and contrast,

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.760
because it's 2D and it's on the skin, right? So I remember my mom

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:14.000
saying, okay, you don't use colors ever, and I'm your mom, I'd like to see some colors in your

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:19.760
drawing. So as I was going back to drawing, I did this. I took it with me. This is the first

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.200
piece I did with some colors, if we can say that. I don't know if you can see, it's blurred.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:32.720
Can I change the blurring thing? Anyway, I'll describe it. This is

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:40.560
the skull of a cat, right? And there is a dollar symbol in it. Okay,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:47.840
you can see it here. Yeah, right here. And then you have the QR code here. Everything is

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.560
done with a ballpoint pen, so it's all black and white.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:54.240
And then I added some gold things because I'm from hip hop culture.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:57.120
I like hip hop, so that was a little reference here.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:01.360
And basically, if you scan this, you end up on the white paper.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.680
Very nice. So that was just a way for me to push the white paper, because that's the

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.200
starting point, I believe, if you're trying to understand Bitcoin. So I believe this piece is

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:19.680
from the middle part, because I started, I think, gold to the black to the white, and you find that

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:27.480
in my recent work as well. You run a node. When did Bitcoin stop being an idea you liked

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:33.680
and become a machine in your house?

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:40.860
From Europe, I would say that basically not being able to transfer my money the way I

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:51.120
wanted to, and to use the money you have in the bank account where you want, the limitations, and just looking for practical solutions.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:55.960
I found Bitcoin along the way, and then you realize that running your node gives you

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:01.020
a lot of options, and it's just about self-custody and sovereignty. And once you figure that out,

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.620
then a lot of things become possible for yourself

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:08.400
in terms of transferring money, basically.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:12.680
Was there a specific moment, or a person, or a bill you

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.520
could not pay that made it obvious?

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:16.880
Yeah.

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:23.160
The turning point for me was really when I was at this fiat job,

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:27.480
and every day in the store, on the floor, and making a lot of

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:33.280
money for these brands, and I had a stroke on the 25th of December.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:37.720
So I was with my family and I'm having a stroke, and I'm less than 30 years

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:38.880
old at this point, right?

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.800
And then I'm like, okay, this is not right.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:51.440
And I cannot be doing this anymore. Because this time it was okay, I recovered, and it wasn't bad. But I don't want to be spending my life in a store with fake lights and then

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:52.640
die, right?

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:57.600
So that was really a wake-up call. And I know a lot of people I've talked to in the Bitcoin community,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.600
some of them had near-death experiences, and they just had this

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.840
out-of-reality

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:15.000
experience that made them think twice, and they just realized that, okay, maybe there is something else I can do, something better, something more aligned I can do with my life. Because more than

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.440
sats, more than Bitcoin,

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.200
the most important currency that we have

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:20.800
is the time that we are given,

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:22.840
and we don't know how much we have.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:26.180
So that's a very humbling experience for me.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:31.180
And that pretty much killed a big part of my ego,

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.920
and I realized that you want to leave something that matters

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.200
and that can help people once you're gone, right?

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.640
So having a stroke and almost dying

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:41.720
made me realize I should pursue Bitcoin,

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:44.120
because I knew about Bitcoin at that point.

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:49.700
And you're just stacking. I was basically DCAing away for almost

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:51.700
five, eight years.

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.200
So you work every day, you stack your 200 bucks, 20 bucks here and there.

00:10:57.500 --> 00:11:01.500
And then, yeah, if you don't get to enjoy that purchasing power,

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:07.680
that energy that you stored, and you die, you basically did that for nothing, right?

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:08.880
Even if you don't die, right?

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:10.680
Let's talk about COVID and what happened, right?

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.760
Like, you can basically just stack a lot of money,

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:15.120
and then everybody goes away,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.480
and then you're left with regrets and questions

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:20.600
and anger and all these emotions, right?

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:24.640
And some people had this money waiting for their kids,

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:27.080
and if you're a husband and you have a wife and

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.760
responsibilities in general, right? So

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:34.320
of course it comes with risk, but what is the other alternative?

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.480
Staying in a fiat job and slowly dying, right? So that was my take here.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:48.840
You said some great things there, and I want to try and just unpack this for a moment, because

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:58.680
I don't think, when we talk about the sound money principles of Bitcoin, that many people

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:07.360
can relate how the fiat system does not only affect your ability to

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:12.800
economize and pay your bills, but it trickles all the way

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:19.560
down to your health and your wellness. And you were able to

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:28.620
make the connection between being under 30 years old and having so much stress or pressure in

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:37.020
your life due to your engagement in a broken system that was stealing your value.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:47.000
And that broken system resulted in you having a serious health scare at a young age.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:58.000
And you look at the cause of that issue as being how you go about taking care of yourself.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:06.260
Not like your food, not like your environment or where you're living, but literally the thing that you wake up

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.240
and you go to work for, and how you store your value

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:11.180
and your time and your energy.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:17.220
That system was literally taking your life in advance,

00:13:18.340 --> 00:13:22.520
and you realized it early enough to say, you know what?

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:25.840
This is the problem. It's affecting not only

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:29.200
my ability to preserve my value,

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.160
but it's also just affecting my ability to just be here

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:35.560
in my best capacity.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.560
And we've got to get to a point where we realize

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:43.160
how this system is destroying not only your ability

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:47.320
to pay your bills, but everything that you're associated with,

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.560
everything about you.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.440
This is why I feel it's so important

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:52.720
that we're doing what we're doing,

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:55.240
and having these conversations, and having you

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.440
and artists come on and explain these things,

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:01.920
and how this fiat system is affecting your life.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:03.560
This is so important.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:07.120
And another step you took was you found your way to

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:18.000
Nostr in 2024. What did you realize the old internet couldn't do for artists that made you

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:28.000
want to come into a freedom tech protocol, to be able to communicate globally with people?

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:35.040
The interesting story is that I joined Nostr and X for the first time at the same time. So around

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:40.560
the same time, I was traveling around Europe for Bitcoin conferences, and I joined both because

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:44.640
my friends were like, okay, you're starting to have some traction and you should be having

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:45.280
socials.

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:48.560
So I tried both. And simple as it is,

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:51.200
I tried both and then I got banned

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:53.340
after two months on X.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.200
So I tried, right?

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.400
I'm not a big social media person.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.600
I try to do it when it comes to professional life

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.160
and all, but when it comes to myself,

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:05.760
I'm not very interested in that.

00:15:11.120 --> 00:15:16.960
So I do it for the right reason, in my opinion. It's time to do it. And then you get banned, and the reason was not explained. I received emails saying I'm shady and I'm just

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:27.160
bad, right? So no way to get the account back or anything. And I believe the reason was that I had just created

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:28.000
the account,

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:30.080
and then I had a lot of famous people

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:31.440
following me or whatever,

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:32.680
and it looked shady, whatever.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:34.760
I didn't have a profile picture.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:39.160
And yeah, so it's more like the choices you make

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:41.040
in your life, and taking care of yourself,

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:42.480
and being in line with your values.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:47.740
But if you get deplatformed, they make the choice for you, basically.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.280
I didn't have to make the choice.

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:51.980
And then I ended up with Nostr, and Nostr has

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:56.140
been the perfect tool, because it's just not censored.

00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:58.460
And it's about freedom of expression.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:03.940
And if I could access my account, I would love to use X more, but I cannot.

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:04.380
Right.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.760
So now we are back on X. Since we created the Ninefold studio,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:10.720
I also have an X account for BLEEP,

00:16:10.720 --> 00:16:12.580
my animated series,

00:16:12.580 --> 00:16:14.800
but I didn't create a new account for myself,

00:16:14.800 --> 00:16:17.440
because at this point, I don't want to have another account.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.320
We have too many accounts already,

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:21.640
and Nostr is doing just great.

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:27.760
So it's just the relay technology allowing you to be censorship resistant.

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:34.480
And that is so important. That is very important. This is what the decentralized

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:44.240
sovereignty tools are giving you: the ability to not be censored, to be in charge of your own work.

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:52.220
And this is so important as centralization becomes more and more regulated.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:57.700
You sell work for sats, including on one website, Take My Sats.

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:04.740
What changed in how you price a piece once the payment became peer to peer?

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:13.840
It becomes more about the value and less about the price tag. I think I had to work on pricing my work. I think it's a thing

00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:18.520
artists go through in general, because you have this piece in your head and you think it's

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:23.680
great, or it's not good enough. And it's hard to price, right? The value is subjective.

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:27.560
So for the pins, I just went for 21k sats,

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:30.120
because I think it's just relevant,

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.200
and it makes sense now.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:32.800
It's potentially going to change,

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:36.680
but most importantly, I am in uncharted territory here,

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:38.560
and I get lost every day.

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:41.280
I look at the new tech and the new things

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:44.800
that are being built by people, by agents, even by themselves.

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.240
And it's amazing, everything that is being built today,

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:51.160
every day, and you have new tools every day

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:53.720
coming out, every single day,

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.480
and it's hard to keep up.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:18:00.280
But I think there is asymmetrical risk in doing this,

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.080
and it's positioning for the long term,

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:03.960
low time preference once again.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:08.420
So Take My Sats is just a direct consequence

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.400
of the relay technology that we discussed.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:14.000
So people realize that on top of relays,

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.060
you can not only share information,

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:19.440
but you can also do a lot of things.

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:21.840
And so Nostr is being more and more developed

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.560
as an ecosystem in which you can plug yourself in

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:31.760
with your keys, and that's very interesting. So Take My Sats is just a shop, and everything is working, and

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:38.320
that's the most important, right? So the pricing part, to get back to this, I believe that

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:47.760
I put my price where it's high, because the low time preference is telling me that I'll be here for a while,

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:53.760
I hope so. And if not, people will have the luxury to price my art in the future, looking back.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:19:00.400
And I believe that if you take a very nice artwork from Michelangelo or Da Vinci, not comparing

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:07.540
myself to them, but I believe it was Da Vinci traveling from Italy to France on a donkey with the Mona Lisa

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:11.840
strapped on the back. Right. So now it's priceless. And if

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:14.720
you asked him at the time, can you trade me that for food,

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:18.520
maybe he would have traded it for food. Right. So I'm not worried

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:21.920
about the pricing. It's really about the value. And if I can

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:25.920
bring a smile and make people think at all, and if

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:30.120
in the future my art is worth more, it's good for the person who owns it at that point.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:35.720
And that's the whole point: to preserve the value and have it go up, always higher.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:38.520
That's the type of artwork we want to build.

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:42.480
But to create those pieces of art, you need to back it with the community, you need to back

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.520
it with your personality.

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:49.600
You cannot just make an art piece and not take a position and not say anything.

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:52.560
You cannot, in my opinion. If you have to express something,

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:56.160
better if it's personal and if it's your vision of the world.

00:19:56.160 --> 00:20:00.480
I'm interested in what's going on with you, Dion, when I'm talking to you.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.280
I'm not interested in you if you're able to copy this or that, right?

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:08.040
So the value, if it's backed by yourself

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:10.480
and you do the proof of work with low time preference,

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:11.920
it will compound over time.

00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:15.160
And with time, we see what works, what doesn't.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:20.160
And so I believe that Bitcoin needs to be

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:23.360
backed by real life assets.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:29.080
And those real life assets need to be in service of the community, and used by the community,

00:20:29.080 --> 00:20:30.600
and owned by the community.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:32.080
Same with the whole thing too,

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:33.080
right?

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:36.680
Including Nostr, including Bitcoin, and all the different tools and technologies we

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:39.160
have.

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:44.240
Your bio says open source enthusiast.

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:49.040
Has building in public made the work better?

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.400
I think it does. It just does.

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.360
Because scrutiny keeps you on your toes

00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:00.320
and gives you feedback, more or less positive.

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:02.280
But if you don't build in public,

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:07.540
it's easy to get caught up in a loop, or in echo chambers, and we see that a lot with AI now.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:14.040
Nowadays you see a lot of people just vibe coding and coming up with things, and they have zero users.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:18.040
I'm not saying they will never have users. I'm just saying, what is the point?

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:24.080
Are you just exploring creation here, and you want to come up with something that you build? In that case, that's great.

00:21:24.400 --> 00:21:28.120
But then, is it a commercial product, right?

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:29.760
What are you trying to achieve?

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:32.440
So in my opinion,

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:35.000
we need to...

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:40.000
I'm not sure. Can you come again?

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:41.240
(laughs)

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:42.720
Yes.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:50.000
We just want to know the difference between building in that open source space and building

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:57.600
for some centralized authority that has now put themselves in between you and the audience.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:00.720
Yeah, that's a more precise question. That's different.

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:11.200
If you don't have a middleman, then you come back to more value, you come back to more direct ecosystems. And the faster knowledge and value travel through Bitcoin and apps, the faster it

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:16.880
travels, and that speed is going to tell you how well your economy is doing and how well the market

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:23.520
is doing. If the money is stuck somewhere, and I know that HODLing is very important for

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:29.560
Bitcoiners, but if the sats are just sitting somewhere and never spent,

00:22:29.560 --> 00:22:30.880
you have a problem.

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:34.920
So we need to accelerate the velocity of money,

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:37.480
of knowledge, of everything, and open source everything.

00:22:37.480 --> 00:22:39.760
Because if it's open source, it's decentralized,

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:42.840
so it's more efficient. There is nothing centralized

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:50.160
that is more efficient than something that is decentralized. The way the Tokyo subway was made, have you heard this story?

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:57.280
Scientists in charge of planning were developing the subway, and they're like, okay,

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:02.960
it's a very big cosmopolitan city. How do we do that? And they took some cells and

00:23:02.960 --> 00:23:07.760
bacteria and viruses and they put them in the lab, and

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:14.640
then the way those bacteria developed was the most efficient. So basically they put some food

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:19.440
where the stations should be, because this is the map of the city, and then they let the bacteria

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:28.800
just connect them. And they reproduced that map that the bacteria made on the city, and that's why the Tokyo

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:33.200
subway is very efficient, even for the size of the city.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:34.200
Interesting.

00:23:34.200 --> 00:23:35.800
Yes, very interesting, right?

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:36.800
Wow.

00:23:36.800 --> 00:23:37.800
Exactly.

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:39.720
And God is decentralized, right?

00:23:39.720 --> 00:23:41.640
So everything is decentralized.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:42.640
You are decentralized.

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:43.640
We are decentralized.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:44.640
Air is decentralized.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:50.000
Water is decentralized. So putting a centralized label on anything is not going to make it centralized.

00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:59.000
At Nostriga in 2024, the alien character that you drew was tattooed on a friend's leg.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:05.840
What did it feel like when a picture left the screen and went onto skin?

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:15.280
I'm always very grateful when I get to tattoo somebody, because I'm still learning. I will not pretend I'm a tattoo artist, because I've never worked in a shop and I've never apprenticed.

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:24.240
But it's a lot of gratefulness. People trust you with something that they will have on their

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.240
body forever. That's a lot of trust.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:34.480
And it's a living medium. You're not drawing on a canvas, right? The skin is

00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:42.960
different depending on the person, depending on the body part. And so it's a very intimate moment,

00:24:42.960 --> 00:24:46.180
always. And that moment was a full moment of trust.

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:49.860
As I said, my friend just wanted to support me, and he already had the tools.

00:24:49.860 --> 00:24:54.500
And so, yeah, grateful. But that's mostly what I feel like when these events and moments

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:59.620
happen at conferences, because it's just full of love and community sharing good vibes,

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:00.620
right?

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:02.860
So very grateful.

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:10.080
GENESIS 113 is your collaboration with Timechain Art Magazine. You describe Bitcoin as

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:17.840
frequency, a tower of light rising out of noise. What did you want someone to feel standing in

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:24.880
front of it? Now, just to correct one thing, it's not a collaboration with Timechain Art Magazine.

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:30.160
Actually, I did the piece, and then it's about to be featured in Timechain Art Magazine,

00:25:30.160 --> 00:25:36.080
Edition One, coming soon. So shout out Asanoha for letting me share my art

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:42.240
in the Genesis Edition. And it's been in Denver, at The Space, for a while now. We've

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:46.880
been sharing the piece. So if you want to see it live, you can go there and see how you feel in front of it.

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:50.200
But I really wanted to do something that

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:52.560
makes people curious.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.320
As I said, I'm really about education.

00:25:54.320 --> 00:25:57.960
I want to make people think. I don't want to put any ideas in your head.

00:25:57.960 --> 00:26:00.120
So that's why my design is quite simple.

00:26:00.520 --> 00:26:03.200
But I'm trying to shock with something, right?

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:06.000
So there is the white and black contrast that, once again,

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:09.200
you can interpret, when it comes to symbolism, in many ways.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:12.200
The eye, the all-seeing eye, that can be seen as many things

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:15.600
depending on history, time, civilization.

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:18.600
And the Bitcoin logo that is, on purpose,

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:21.200
put there big and bold, because I want people,

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:22.200
if they are Bitcoiners,

00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:24.960
maybe they will interpret it depending on what they know.

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:28.400
But I'm also more and more targeting no-coiners,

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:31.400
and what we call shitcoiners,

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:33.420
because I think there are many ways

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:35.960
you can do crypto, sure, whatever.

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:38.040
But eventually, if you want to be serious about it,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:39.360
there is only Bitcoin, right?

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:44.360
So, yeah, I would say go think about it yourself.

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:49.720
What do I want you to feel? Actually, I want you to feel something,

00:26:49.720 --> 00:26:54.160
because I think we are so alienated and traumatized.

00:26:54.160 --> 00:26:57.960
So if you can just have 20 seconds of meditation

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:00.240
or self-reflection in front of a piece of mine,

00:27:00.240 --> 00:27:02.120
or anybody else's, that would be great.

00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:08.520
You brought the GENESIS 113 pins to BTC Prague,

00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:14.820
then to Plebeian Market. Who was that object for: the collector, or the person who just

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:20.140
wants to carry the signal? So, always for the people. I do this for the

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:25.000
plebs. So of course I want more and more people to wear these pins,

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.320
so that they can recognize each other.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:30.240
But that pin was a collaboration

00:27:30.240 --> 00:27:32.960
with Modeotec Art, an OG Bitcoin artist.

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:36.240
So after he saw the painting, he contacted me

00:27:36.240 --> 00:27:38.600
and was basically saying, yo, I like your style.

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:42.800
That's somebody I met in real life back in 2024 in Europe.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:46.480
So we were just discussing the design and the painting, and I was sharing

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:52.000
with him the video, because there is a music video coming with the art piece. And so he was like,

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:58.240
oh, let's do a pin, because he's working with PCB art. And I modified the design, I created a

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:09.920
new file for him, and then he did that beautiful PCB art pin. That is a limited edition, I believe it has 42 pieces.

00:28:09.920 --> 00:28:13.520
So yeah, it launched at BTC Prague.

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:17.200
If you don't know his work, you should definitely check it out.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:20.000
And the quality of these pins is amazing.

00:28:20.000 --> 00:28:23.320
So that's more a little piece of art than anything.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:26.240
And of course, the collection aspect of it,

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:27.640
as we mentioned earlier,

00:28:27.640 --> 00:28:30.120
we want to build on top of everything that we are doing.

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:33.000
So we've got low time preference in mind.

00:28:33.000 --> 00:28:36.120
So as we go, each piece is going to build on top

00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:38.240
of the others. The worlds are going to communicate

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:39.960
and connect with each other.

00:28:39.960 --> 00:28:42.920
So expect collectibles, and more and more

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:44.680
value compounding, right?

00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:47.360
Yes, we love Modeotec.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:51.920
I love how you're connecting with all of these artists that are doing some great things

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.000
in the Bitcoin space.

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:54.840
This is really awesome.

00:28:54.840 --> 00:28:57.080
I can't wait to see what else you guys are making.

00:28:57.760 --> 00:28:58.360
Thank you.

00:28:58.360 --> 00:29:07.840
The animated series follows an extraterrestrial trying to understand humanity through Bitcoin. I will say this is probably

00:29:07.840 --> 00:29:14.800
my favorite of your work. You have said that it's about the signal, consciousness,

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:24.800
and the internet. Who is the show actually for? Everybody. And I would say it started, I did this for

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:27.280
me, because I'm

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:32.000
very interested in symbolism and language, and I'm a little bit autistic, so I had a hard

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:37.760
time communicating with people in my life, because of the words lost in translation,

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:50.400
meaning lost in translation, and being very visual. So I did it for myself, and as a way to leave a trace of the way I've been

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:56.520
experiencing those conferences and the community in general. So that's basically,

00:29:56.520 --> 00:30:02.720
not that the alien is me, but I gave him a few traits that are me, like the awkwardness

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:05.200
sometimes, and "I don't know what I'm doing here,"

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:12.320
right? So I think it's for everyone. It's for everyone. Consciousness is for everyone. And the alien is just

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:17.600
asking you the question, like, where is Bitcoin coming from? Where are we coming from? If an alien

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:23.680
coming from outside of planet Earth, as consciousness, is able to interact with Bitcoin and understand

00:30:23.680 --> 00:30:25.320
Bitcoin, maybe even better

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:29.580
than we do, what does it say about us? What does it say about Bitcoin? And just wonder

00:30:29.580 --> 00:30:35.280
about these things. And then you can also do specific episodes to bring attention to

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:41.360
one topic or the other. So it's just fun so far, and I'm glad that you are enjoying it.

00:30:41.360 --> 00:30:43.360
Thank you.

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:46.720
You've also said children could learn proof of work

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:52.480
from an alien watching miners. What does the story teach

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:59.040
that a lecture may not teach? I've organized kids' zones in the past, in El Salvador,

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:04.120
in Vegas. So you just run this space for kids and you try to do activities, and

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:09.080
the best way people, and especially kids, learn is through compassion and participation, right?

00:31:09.080 --> 00:31:14.840
First, in the case of my animated series, you make them participate, because when we are watching a show,

00:31:14.840 --> 00:31:18.880
we are basically living through the main character's eyes.

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:22.560
And whatever the character is going through, through compassion, you're feeling it too.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:25.040
So I think having

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:30.080
relatable characters, more and more, in stories, we are more and more

00:31:30.080 --> 00:31:35.120
digging into the main character vibe, and character building and world

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:40.320
building, when it comes to animation and films. And so if you have a really good

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:45.280
character, you can take over the world, just like Mickey, right? Everybody can

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:50.000
relate to it, and then it doesn't matter if he has a little logo on his forehead.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:54.960
The alien is the catchy part, and then you figure out he has a Bitcoin logo, maybe,

00:31:54.960 --> 00:32:00.560
if you realize it, and then, what is this on his forehead? So it's really about,

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:04.880
I'm very straightforward like that. So when I see a problem, I always ask myself, is it a problem?

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:11.600
Is it an easy problem, or can I just walk around it? And with a nice little alien

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:15.920
and animation, I can bypass the fact that people sometimes are scared of me because of my tattoos.

00:32:15.920 --> 00:32:22.320
So yeah, I'm just finding ways to navigate. And the most important part is I want to relate.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:25.520
I'm full of compassion. I want people to connect to that part

00:32:25.520 --> 00:32:29.040
of themselves, their humanness, and I want them to connect with people, because we need

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:35.680
community, we need each other, just to heal from the trauma and all the fiat things that

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:42.240
happened to us, and that we actually gave our consent to. Not blaming anybody, but you have to save

00:32:42.240 --> 00:32:45.040
yourself, and you can only save yourself. And first and foremost,

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:50.320
that's what you should be doing. So if a little alien can be inspiring you to do so,

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:57.600
or whatever else, I'm fine with it. You and Ivy Lumi announced Ninefold at Origin Seoul.

00:32:58.400 --> 00:33:06.640
Eight worlds, including the series, Life with Bitcoin, Zaza, Tynka. What is the studio for, in one sentence?

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:16.880
The line is "stories that make you build." So that's what we do, but I would say that our

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:26.520
goal is to bridge. Bridge worlds, communities, continents, anything, because we are so separated from ourselves,

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:29.040
as I just mentioned, but also from each other.

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:32.560
And so, the ability that, instead of focusing

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:34.760
on what makes us different from each other,

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:37.560
skin color, tattoos or not, and all these things,

00:33:37.560 --> 00:33:40.120
we can just focus on the common ground.

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:42.760
And the common ground can be with Bitcoin,

00:33:42.760 --> 00:33:46.400
once we realize that, okay, you do art, I do

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:50.560
art too. Then maybe we can do a podcast, just like we're doing now, and talk about this,

00:33:50.560 --> 00:33:55.280
even if we don't have much context about each other. We just had a quick call

00:33:55.280 --> 00:34:00.560
to connect. But I could be focusing on something else, like, I don't know him, where is he coming

00:34:00.560 --> 00:34:06.760
from? And it's just a matter of perspective. And so I'm really about bridging, and I think that's what

00:34:06.760 --> 00:34:10.000
we are trying to portray with our different worlds.

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:13.520
And it's designed so that it can grow.

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:15.920
The way that the website has been designed by Zaziz

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:18.360
is so that if tomorrow you tell me,

00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:20.880
depending on what you want, you want to join Ninefold,

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:23.200
you tell me, I tell Zaziz, we change the website,

00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:24.240
and you join it.

00:34:24.240 --> 00:34:48.840
So it's not a contract. It's about you joining and participating in the effort and the proof of work. It comes from the experience that both Ivy Lumi and I have had in the Bitcoin industry. She's more from event production in Bitcoin.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:51.680
And she was seeing all these artists, like me

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:53.760
and my friends, just not selling.

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:55.240
And I realized the same thing.

00:34:55.240 --> 00:34:57.080
And I wanted to have my friends sell more

00:34:57.080 --> 00:34:58.440
and represent themselves.

00:34:58.440 --> 00:35:00.520
And we realized that, yes,

00:35:00.520 --> 00:35:03.960
Bitcoin is about no middleman and peer to peer.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:08.080
But people can be middlemen for their friends

00:35:08.080 --> 00:35:10.960
and help them. It's just a matter of intention

00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:11.800
and how you do that.

00:35:11.800 --> 00:35:15.960
If I'm trying to set you up and take 50% of your profit,

00:35:15.960 --> 00:35:16.800
it's not cool.

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:18.320
But if I'm actually helping you,

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:19.560
and then you're giving value back,

00:35:19.560 --> 00:35:23.280
and we can negotiate ways to pay each other value for value,

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:25.440
and we can bring the product and the

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:29.680
experience to life, because that's the point. We want to get analog with it. We want

00:35:29.680 --> 00:35:34.560
to have real life experiences. Once you have these experiences, once the cash flow is coming,

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:38.560
you can always find ways to make everybody happy. I believe it's about making

00:35:38.560 --> 00:35:43.680
the cake bigger, and we should stop the predatory behavior that we have learned in the

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:44.680
fiat system.

00:35:48.240 --> 00:35:55.600
That's great, what you just said. There are a lot of tools that are now being built for us to do just what you mentioned,

00:35:55.600 --> 00:36:03.040
about others being able to help artists in a way where it's not about them,

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:06.880
but it's about helping put those artists in a position

00:36:06.880 --> 00:36:10.000
where they can create their own value.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:12.920
And one of those tools that Bitcoin For The Arts

00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:16.560
is partnering with is Fanfares.

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:19.520
And it does precisely what you just said.

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:24.200
It allows creators to receive direct value.

00:36:24.200 --> 00:36:27.440
And then if you are a supporter or a friend

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:32.800
of those creators, you can share their content and receive a portion of the

00:36:32.800 --> 00:36:38.480
value for sharing, while also promoting that creator and allowing them to

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:44.160
receive more value. So these are the things that are now being built and are

00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:45.200
coming our way.

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:51.600
So there is so much to look forward to, and opportunities, in this decentralized space.

00:36:51.600 --> 00:36:56.400
The studio line: stories that make you build.

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:01.400
What does that ask of a person watching?

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:02.200
I get it.

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:07.280
It's almost like you're inviting people to open their minds

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:13.480
and be inspired to collaborate and build in a space that gives them the opportunity to

00:37:13.480 --> 00:37:15.960
do that. Is that what we're doing here with this?

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:21.620
Yeah, basically. I think we were mentioning the common ground, right? And the oldest common

00:37:21.620 --> 00:37:28.320
ground we all have is stories. We are all humans and we all come from this storytelling place,

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:31.520
sitting around the fire and some old man telling you the story.

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:34.560
It doesn't matter if you're from here or there.

00:37:34.560 --> 00:37:37.920
All the civilizations across time have been doing this.

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:44.560
And we talk to each other and share knowledge that way.

00:37:44.560 --> 00:37:47.000
And the story is more than just the words.

00:37:47.000 --> 00:37:52.000
It's the personal interpretation of the people who actually lived it.

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:57.000
So there is also a little bit of their experience and emotions and fear,

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:00.000
but we also feel that through compassion, as I mentioned earlier, right?

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.000
So when you're doing that, you're listening to that old man,

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:05.520
and these things that happened

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:12.560
60 years ago, right? You were there too. Through him, you were there. You don't have to live through

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:17.680
Nazi Germany to know that it happened. Because in Europe, I have had people in my family telling me

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:26.960
what it was like to be under Nazi occupation, right? So you can either dismiss it and say, all right, it was a long time ago,

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:31.760
and it's done and it's gone forever. Or you can be like, okay, this is something that happened once,

00:38:31.760 --> 00:38:37.440
and history tends to repeat itself. So what can I learn from it? How can I grow from it? And as an

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:44.000
individual, in the new context that we are in, of course, we are not in the 50s anymore. But what is

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:45.720
the new context, and what can I do?

00:38:45.720 --> 00:38:48.440
And how can I outgrow myself?

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:50.800
How can I be better, for the reasons we mentioned?

00:38:50.800 --> 00:38:53.920
For everybody, it says, the minimum thing you can do

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:57.120
is respect, at the minimum, the people that are around you.

00:38:57.120 --> 00:38:59.040
You want to be taking a shower.

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:01.040
You want to be presenting yourself.

00:39:01.040 --> 00:39:04.120
It's okay to be coming up short

00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:05.520
and not be all of it,

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:10.320
whatever you think you are. But the story is what matters, because the story you're repeating

00:39:10.320 --> 00:39:14.000
to yourself in your head is your reality. So that's where I'm coming from.

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:24.720
You also produce events. What does a Bitcoin room owe the artist who shows up to work in it?

00:39:24.720 --> 00:39:27.200
And I need to know this, really,

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:32.800
personally, as well as an arts organization, because we want to position ourselves

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:37.200
where we're showcasing these artists, and we want to make sure that these

00:39:37.200 --> 00:39:43.120
artists are getting what they deserve, or feel that they're in a position in that

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:46.000
room to be treated the way they deserve.

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:53.000
It starts with respect, right? When it comes to galleries or anything in life, respect is respect.

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:58.000
If you don't respect me, we're going to have a hard time talking. And it goes both ways, right?

00:39:58.000 --> 00:40:06.320
So the fiat way of doing art galleries is just the art galleries making money, because it's a fiat game and it's profit

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:08.200
and whatever.

00:40:08.200 --> 00:40:12.120
And so the artist needs the gallery to sell.

00:40:14.840 --> 00:40:18.200
But the power dynamics are reversed,

00:40:18.200 --> 00:40:20.920
because the art gallery needs the artist more

00:40:20.920 --> 00:40:22.000
than the other way around.

00:40:22.000 --> 00:40:24.000
If you have an art gallery and no artist,

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:25.840
you have white walls at best.

00:40:26.880 --> 00:40:29.480
And you still need a painter to paint your walls white.

00:40:29.480 --> 00:40:33.520
So once again, we can be sitting in offices.

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:36.360
And as I said, I come from finance,

00:40:36.360 --> 00:40:37.440
economics, marketing.

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:39.280
I was supposed to be working an office job

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:43.160
and give orders and make way more money

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:45.000
than I have made, right?

00:40:45.080 --> 00:40:46.720
But that wasn't for me.

00:40:46.720 --> 00:40:51.400
And I think we should realize, sooner or later,

00:40:51.400 --> 00:40:54.280
that creativity is the real power.

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:59.280
And then as the artist, being able to create something out

00:40:59.280 --> 00:41:03.720
of yourself and put it into the world,

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.120
you have more power than the gallery.

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:07.680
The gallery comes downstream. You're upstream.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:11.520
I think it's about coming back to common sense,

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:14.560
and not putting emotions or

00:41:14.560 --> 00:41:18.240
greed into the process of all this. And just,

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:21.480
what does common sense tell you right now?

00:41:21.480 --> 00:41:23.800
The artist is creating first,

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:31.800
then you are supposed to serve the artist, help the artist, and your profit will be made because you serve them well.

00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:39.800
And we need to bring that selfless way of being around people back, because that's who we are.

00:41:40.520 --> 00:41:45.000
If I do my best, every day, my best to make Bitcoin For The Arts great,

00:41:45.000 --> 00:41:49.000
and you, every day you wake up and you're like, okay, I'm going to do my best for you,

00:41:49.000 --> 00:41:53.000
I think of you, you think of me, more than we think about ourselves,

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:57.000
you'll be surprised at the type of dynamics you can create, and the type of products,

00:41:57.000 --> 00:42:00.000
and the type of things that can happen if you do that.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:04.000
It's once again the story, and the perspective you have on

00:42:04.000 --> 00:42:06.500
life. And if you come from fear, then you will be

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:11.660
separating yourself from people, and from opportunities as well.

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:18.600
Five years from now: the studio, the series, the objects, and you.

00:42:19.120 --> 00:42:23.380
Where do you want to be, and where does Bitcoin sit inside that picture?

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:27.680
If you had asked me two or three years ago

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:35.040
where I would be, I wouldn't have said where I am now. So, very fast moving environment. And as we said,

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:40.480
every day is changing, and I have no idea where I will be in five years, or where we will be. But I know

00:42:40.480 --> 00:42:46.340
that I've become more and more selfless and working for Bitcoin.

00:42:47.720 --> 00:42:50.520
And I work for Bitcoin.

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:55.080
Bitcoin has the ethos, as the protocol, right?

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:58.720
So what can I do for Bitcoin?

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:00.200
That's what I ask myself.

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:03.560
And then if you do that, you're sorted,

00:43:03.560 --> 00:43:04.960
you're totally sorted.

00:43:04.960 --> 00:43:10.560
Yeah, there's nothing else you should be worrying about, other than how much can you give? How much can you give,

00:43:10.560 --> 00:43:15.920
and can you give more? And how can you make that sustainable for yourself, and balance it? And then

00:43:15.920 --> 00:43:22.560
you have it. Just stay there, and don't worry, just be healthy, keep building. It's a fine balance, a fine

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:25.480
line, between having low time preference and being

00:43:25.480 --> 00:43:29.160
disciplined enough to show up every day and do proof of work, right?

00:43:29.160 --> 00:43:35.800
So if you just do that, efforts compound every day, and in five years we'll see, right?

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:37.760
I love that, man.

00:43:37.760 --> 00:43:40.560
What can you do for Bitcoin?

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:47.200
That's a completely different mindset than being inside the traditional system.

00:43:47.200 --> 00:43:54.800
And this is what we are doing. It's pushing these stories out so people can understand

00:43:54.800 --> 00:44:01.400
that Bitcoin ethos. So you are doing some great work for Bitcoin just by sharing your

00:44:01.400 --> 00:44:09.600
story. Last question, we ask every guest this. If a $500 to $2,000

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:14.960
Bitcoin micro-grant from Bitcoin For The Arts landed in your wallet tomorrow, what would

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:22.160
it unlock for you as a creator? We are building worlds, and we will keep building worlds.

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:25.120
I like to work with constraints.

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:29.840
So if I get some money from Bitcoin For The Arts,

00:44:29.840 --> 00:44:31.880
I will keep creating, and I will just make sure

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:35.160
that I maximize the use of that money

00:44:35.160 --> 00:44:38.720
and have the biggest impact I can, to involve more people

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:42.960
in the loop and create more community-based activities,

00:44:42.960 --> 00:44:45.440
experiences, merch, just like everything

00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:47.440
we've been building so far with Ninefold.

00:44:47.440 --> 00:44:49.640
But if I have to be precise about it,

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:53.000
BLEEP: Signal is over now for the first season.

00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:55.600
So we just ended, wrapped up nicely.

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:57.560
It's been very well received.

00:44:57.560 --> 00:45:01.840
Working on season two, that will be coming out in 2027.

00:45:01.840 --> 00:45:08.000
And I cannot share now, but it will premiere at some event, and I'm building

00:45:08.000 --> 00:45:13.000
around this premiere. And so if I had some money from you, I would probably maximize everything

00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:18.720
to push that next step for BLEEP, and that will come with nice merch that is already going

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:22.240
on, and a nice premiere for everybody to enjoy.

